<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Béla Kun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Béla Kun was a Hungarian communist who led the Hungarian Soviet Republic of March to August 1919. A former prisoner of war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, he founded the Hungarian Communist Party in November 1918, and his regime nationalised industry and land and carried out a Red Terror. After its fall he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then Soviet Russia, where he was executed in Stalin's purges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bela-kun.jpg" alt="Béla Kun" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gyula Jelfy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bela_Kun_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Béla Kun was born Béla Kohn on 20 February 1886 in the village of Lele, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. His father was a lapsed Jewish village notary, and his mother, a Hungarian, had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> on marriage. He studied law at Franz Joseph University in Kolozsvár from 1904, the year he began to be called Kun rather than Kohn, and befriended the poet Endre Ady, who introduced him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>'s left-wing intelligentsia. He worked as a journalist until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, served in the Austro-Hungarian Army and was captured by the Russian army in 1916.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a> freed him from a prisoner-of-war camp. In March 1918 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he co-founded the Hungarian Group of the Russian Communist Party and came to know Lenin, though he stood with the ultra-radical left, which wished to continue the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>. He fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 16 November 1918 with Soviet money. On 24 November he founded the Party of Communists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and campaigned against the government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihaly-karolyi">Mihály Károlyi</a>.</p><p>The Hungarian Soviet Republic was proclaimed on 21 March 1919, after his release from prison. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandor-garbai">Sándor Garbai</a> was its nominal head, but power rested with Kun, officially People's Commissar for Foreign Affairs and from April also for Defence. Social Democrats held most commissariats but accepted the leadership of the smaller Communist Party. Four months later the regime collapsed before an offensive by the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kun went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where he was interned until July 1920. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> he led the Crimean Revolutionary Committee and took part in the Red Terror in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1920-1921. He advised the German Communists in the failed March Action of 1921, which Lenin blamed him for. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> he was arrested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> on 28 June 1937, accused of Trotskyism and sentenced to death after a brief secret trial. He was shot at Kommunarka on 29 August 1938. After his rehabilitation in 1956 the Soviet party told the Hungarians that he had died in prison in 1939; only in 1989 did the Soviet government acknowledge the true date.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Béla Kun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Béla Kun</b>'s communist government launched the &quot;Red Terror&quot; and drew Hungary into a war with Romania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It returned to the field on 10 November 1918, and in 1919 its troops occupied Budapest and ended <b>Béla Kun</b>'s regime.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Social Democrats merged with the Communist Party of <b>Béla Kun</b>, recently released from prison, and on 21 March 1919 they proclaimed a soviet republic, governed by a Revolutionary Governing Council nominally chaired by Sándor Garbai.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-soviet-republic">Hungarian Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cehu Silvaniei, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.41,23.18&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Cehu Silvaniei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5092 501 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Béla Kun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5542 771h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5542 771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.41,23.18&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
